 options have been ablation  orally  which are irreversible but now there's a  new neuro technology deep brain  stimulation or DBS which can be applied  to those patients who are medically  refractory now we've been hearing a lot  about DBS in the news it's been called  brain hacking it's been given a very  large growth in the global market  there's these amazing surgeries that are  being reported and that the demand is  growing in the field of neurology and  then we're talking about these like  miraculous recoveries for patients who  have Parkinson's and since that was so  effective and people started extending  it to psychiatric indications like  depression and OCD autism etc and it's  even been extended now past that to  disorders like stroke and now even the  US based program is involved so what is  DBS it's a revolutionary new therapy in  which a subcutaneously implanted pulse  generation generator sends continuous  high-frequency stimulation to deep  structures in the brain via chronically  implanted electrodes the deep brain  stimulation device has often been called  a brain pacemaker and it looks like this  it's made of a lead wire with electrodes  the lead wires are being pointed to by  the blue arrows and the electrodes are  circled in red there's an extension wire  to which they're connected and that's  connected to the implantable pulse  generator which then will deliver the  electrical pulses that will go to the  deep targets in the brain that are  specific for whatever the disorder is  the goal of DBS is to rebalance or  modulate the neural circuits  sufficiently to shift the patient into  their best functional state the  mechanism of action of DBS is suggested  to provide therapeutic relief of  refractory symptoms via the modulation  of dysregulated networks but in fact  we don't really understand the  mechanisms or the principles of DBS yet  which actually matches our neurological  disorders we also don't really know how  these disorders what the dysfunction is  that underlies these disorders we have  some ideas but those also have not been  fully determined yet but we do think  that they have to do with these central  circuits that involve the cortex the  basal ganglia and the thalamus and this  is a circuit that goes around in a loop  and what's really important is to keep a  good balance between those pathways that  are in that lumen  so these pathways are signaling each  other through synchronized oscillatory  activity at different frequencies and  it's really sensitive the circuit to  those frequencies and it's thought that  a change in these oscillatory frequency  might be responsible for the disruption  for the change in the behavior that is  reflecting a disruption in the  synchronous activity now DBS has been  approved for Parkinson's essential  tremor and dystonia these were the  original ones for which DBS was approved  and motor disorders and psychiatric  disorders make up neuropsychiatric  disorders and the motor dispersion of  the first ones to be approved and then  it was extended to obsessive compulsive  disorder in the psychiatric psychiatric  disorders category and it's being used  off-label or has been used off-label for  Tourette's syndrome addiction and also  autism and other disorders so we're  going to concentrate today on Tourette's  syndrome  what is Tourette syndrome juditha  Tourette's syndrome is classified by its  symptoms it is classified through  childhood onset multiple the presence of  multiple motor tics and at least one  fawning tic and all of these have to be  persistent for at least a year and in  the absence of some other  medical issue or pharmaceutical  involvement  so using DBS we'd like to be able to  take the opportunity to understand  perhaps the pathophysiology underlying  Tourette's syndrome and maybe see if  there's a role for oscillations and  ultimately see if we might be able to  find a biomarker that would help us  therapeutically so in our lab back in  Florida what we did was use this mirror  paste device which has these electrodes  now circled in red into deep target and  then we implanted this impulse generator  in the cranium the study design was for  five subjects to her treatment  refractory with steel the Lateran  syndrome we did chronic recordings using  the nervous system so these recordings  occurred over time while the patient was  implanted and moving around freely and  behaving the implants are in the central  media nucleus of the thalamus recorded  local field potentials and we then used  the clinical metric the yellow global  tic severity scale or the Y GTS s to  assess symptomatology and what we found  was that there was that there were  clinical benefits correlated with an  increase in the gamma man power  so the oscillatory frequency that we  found was relevant for Tourette Syndrome  and we found one that was was in fact  gamma and what we ended up finding was  that there was a hypo synchrony that was  correlated with the pathophysiology so  on the left you can see our five  patients and the various frequencies  that were recorded and on the right you  can see those same five patients after  six months and you can see that the  responders are the ones in the center  will have this spike in the gala  frequency  range so an increase in gamma was  correlated with a decrease in  symptomatology as reflected on the yg  TSS and when we looked at the other  frequencies we did not see a correlation  with symptomatology which which you can  also see here so we found that there was  an increase in thalamic gamma  synchronization that correlated with an  decrease in symptomatology there was a  clinical correlation between gamma  oscillations and the symptomatology as I  said and that other oscillatory  frequencies did not directly correlate  with clinical benefit the findings  derived from this research have offered  important insights into tick Genesis and  tick expression because up until then we  really didn't have any understanding of  what was underlying to Genesis and now  we can correlate that with a hypo  synchronization of gamma oscillations in  the CM thalamus and that impacts patient  care and of course patient quality of  life when we're able to effectively  apply that in deep brain stimulation  therapy so we could modulate the gamma  band activity in long-term deep brain  stimulation of the central media nucleus  of the thalamus and use that to mitigate  a pathophysiology that's associated with  Tourette's syndrome and that gap event  oscillations couldn't in fact serve as a  biomarker for Tourette's syndrome and  could be useful in online neurosurgical  procedure to help guide the neuro  surgeon to the optimized target location  and as well in closed loop stimulation  now  we wanted even though we have a lot of  advantages and opportunities that we  gained from using the brain stimulation  we also have to consider the ethics so  there's two things to consider when  we're doing ethical considerations one  is deep brain stimulation itself and  what's associated with that and the  other is your blood rehearsal so what do  I mean by that well with deep brain  stimulation  of course there's a risk of surgery and  all the risks that are associated with  having brain surgery including  hemorrhages etc there can be or  epileptic seizures we also have to  consider that we do not understand the  mechanism of action when stimulation and  while it's touted as being reversible  unlike our other ablation therapy and  lesion there be what we don't really  know is what how much plasticity is  involved when we do provide deep brain  stimulation and now it's been suggested  that we do in fact see some plasticity  so that's also debated so the  reversibility of is debated and of  course there are the side effects that  come from the specific target and the  stimulation parameters that are applied  so we can have various side effects that  come actually from the deep brain  stimulation in the therapeutic stage  itself now for the la Tourette syndrome  we have to consider patient selection  age is a very important topic here and  also the clinical presentation we want  to look at target selection because  target selection is also a hotly debated  issue in deep brain stimulation therapy  which extends across all the  neurological disorders to which its  applied Parkinson's Tourette's OCD etc  finding the optimal deep target in the  brain and the optimal region of that  target as well and each of those will  give a specific outcome and for Tourette  Syndrome we also want to look at the  natural history which I'll discuss with  you in a minute and we have considered  it but it's rarely shown in its pure  profile and often is found comorbid with  other disorders especially  obsessive-compulsive disorder and ADHD  so here we can see that in a study that  has looked at the baseline values in  very large abs cohorts that that the  onset the age of onset for Tourette's  was approximately seven point eight  years but the diagnosis occurred at  twelve point three years and then the  surgery occurred at two point twenty  nine years and one of the things that  was important to consider as I mentioned  is the comorbidities and the  comorbidities for treads are often would  drive the patient to actually seek  medical care so it's the expression of  the symptoms of those comorbidities that  are really important and provide a  complex profile for the patient and that  also effect what we see here as the  various targets that are available for  the application of DES so one of the  most compelling ethical issues here is  at what age do we perform DBS on a  patient as you saw the average age was  29 even though the onset age was 7 and  the diagnosis was 12 well this is  relevant when we look at the natural  history of Tourette's syndrome here on  the x-axis we look at the age of the  patient and on the y-axis we can see the  relative tic severity and if you look  you will see that the tic severity is  peaking just pre-pubertal II now that's  a very sensitive time psychologically  and physiologically for people for  children these are sensitive periods in  which a lot of changes are occurring and  so you really want to ask yourself do  you want to interfere at that point with  the child's development if you're going  to have a spontaneous resolution of the  symptoms do you really want to interfere  with the child and do brain surgery this  is very important question to ask  normally it would just jump to the  conclusion and say well I shouldn't  interfere because the there  good chance that the symptoms were  resolved spontaneously but let me remind  you that ticks can present themselves in  various ways and including self  injurious behavior which can be very  damaging but not withstanding that when  you see someone who's barking and  yelping and jumping up and down you  would imagine that they are mentally  incompetent which is not the case for  Tourette's patients in fact they are  completely competent and are would like  to engage as just possible in normal  activity but that becomes really  confounded by the ticks and also by  these various comorbidities that can  occur with juvenile Tourette's syndrome  and so especially in severe cases where  you have things like self injurious  behavior you can really have a lot of  consequences to these symptoms including  physical injury emotional distress you  can have obstruction of Education  because the children have a hard time  attending school whether it's for  concentration or because the other kids  are giving them a hard time  and you also have the psychosocial  impairment that goes with that so you  can do irreparable harm by the time you  get to young adulthood because of all  these other issues that occur so why  would you consider DBS for juveniles  well you would want it emotional  problems that can occur and prevent them  you certainly want to give the best  chance for normal social development to  the child and of course an academic  development that is unimpeded and these  children because of these issues can  develop low self-esteem and finally mood  or anxiety disorders and so these are  very serious problems that absolutely  affect their development well why  wouldn't you want to consider DBS for  juveniles because you're implanting a  device in a developing brain that as we  said  we don't know exactly how it works and  we're not sure of the plasticity issues  etc we have limited knowledge of the  long-term effects these have not been  studied well and certainly not in  juveniles for who have gotten DBS the  devices are very quickly evolving this  is new neuro technology and continually  changing and so you want to be  considered of that as well and there  might be a competing desire for the  implant you don't know if the child  really wants it or if they're competent  to say they do and what if by the time  they're 18 they don't now you really  have an issue about having what another  brain surgery to remove the device and  you know financial burden for doing it  there's a lot of issues that are then  correlated with that and finally when we  consider the Natural History as we said  well there's a really good chance that  there's going to be a spontaneous  resolution so do we want to interfere  with the developing brain by doing brain  surgery and all the risks that are  associated with brain surgery for the  juvenile so what's the bottom line well  really need to sit and think about the  risks versus the benefits and these have  to really be tailored to each individual  patient and considered by a group there  has to be a team that is involved and  there has to be a team that's involved  in the decision-making and it has to be  very carefully considered and it would  be very beneficial to have a neuro  ethicist on that team to help guide the  team through all these different issues  that can come up and are really quite  intricate I'd like to thank the team in  Florida that we did the work together  for the data that I showed you  previously and finally I'd like to thank  you very much for your attendance and as  a reminder I'd like to let you know that  if you have any questions about  presentation and please go ahead and  send them in and I will answer you by  email stay tuned for a next track  neuroethics where we will hear from our  top experts on the ethics of using new  neuro technologies and the impacts of  our advancements to ensure these  objectives continue with best practices  hope to see you there

